  "reason": "With no prior evidence, the highest-value first experiment is to establish the simplest, most interpretable baseline: RNA-only with a single profile and PCA (n=2000) encoding. This configuration has no aggregation, fusion, or image components, so its validation accuracy provides a clean reference point against which every subsequent design choice (image modality, learned encoders like scVI/scGPT, multiprofile aggregation, and multimodal fusion) can be measured. Knowing the RNA-PCA single-profile accuracy first lets me (a) determine whether adding an image modality or a more complex RNA encoder actually improves performance, (b) calibrate how much headroom exists for the remaining 9 experiments, and (c) avoid over-investing in complex multimodal or multiprofile variants if the simple baseline is already strong. It is the single experiment that reduces the most uncertainty about the entire search space."
}

